Subject: [#94-1-546] SEMANTICS:  Faith

Aha! Cracks appear in the extropian dogma of pure reason!
As little as we might like the idea that
"We can never REALLY 'know' anything",
it is the sad truth.  Absolute, objective knowledge is
impossible.  Given that, however, we still have to live
in the real world.  The question is not whether induction,
deduction or any other route to knowledge is 'correct'
or 'reliable' in any absolute sense, but whether it is
USEFUL.  We have Robert Anton Wilson on our reading list;
I think many of us would benefit from a more careful
reading of his work, especially
_The New Inquisition: Irrational Rationalism and the
Citadel of Science_, in which he addresses precisely
this issue.

And now for my heresy: since reason is not ultimately
'true' but merely useful (very much so and often),
how about admitting the usefulness of other paths
to knowledge, like MYSTICISM?

I can hear the anguished screams already...
there is no closed mind like that of a dogmatic
rationalist :-)
